  <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a bone marrow transplant a surgery?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The stem cells are given through a vein, like a blood transfusion. There is no operation. Collecting stem cells from a donor is also usually done from the blood."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a bone marrow transplant painful?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The transplant itself is not painful. Some patients have discomfort from mouth sores, nausea or tiredness caused by conditioning chemotherapy, and these are treated with medicines."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the success rate of bone marrow transplant?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the disease, its stage, the patient’s age and health, and the donor match. Results are best when the transplant is done early. For example, children with thalassemia who receive a matched sibling transplant early have high cure rates. Your doctor can explain the expected outcome for your condition."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who can be a bone marrow donor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A healthy brother or sister with a full HLA match is usually the first choice. A parent, child or sibling with a half match, or a matched unrelated volunteer from a registry, can also donate."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it safe to donate bone marrow or stem cells?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Donation is safe for healthy donors. Most donors give stem cells from the blood by apheresis, which needs no surgery, and the body replaces the cells within a few weeks."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does the bone marrow transplant process take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Evaluation and preparation take a few weeks. The hospital stay is usually about 2 to 3 weeks for an autologous transplant and 4 to 6 weeks for a donor transplant. Close follow-up continues for the first 100 days and beyond."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the best age for a bone marrow transplant in thalassemia?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Results are best when the transplant is done in early childhood, before iron overload damages the liver and heart. Families should get siblings HLA-tested soon after diagnosis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"GVHD happens when the donor’s immune cells attack the patient’s body, such as the skin, gut or liver. It occurs only after a donor transplant and is treated with immune-suppressing medicines."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does a bone marrow transplant cost in Bangalore?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The cost depends on the type of transplant, the donor, the hospital stay and any complications. A donor transplant usually costs more than an autologous transplant. The clinic can share an estimate after evaluation. Eligible families may get support through schemes such as Coal India’s Thalassemia Bal Sewa Yojana, which gives up to ₹10 lakh for thalassemia major and aplastic anemia transplants (annual family income below ₹8 lakh)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a bone marrow transplant cure blood cancer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A donor transplant can cure some leukemias and other blood cancers. An autologous transplant helps control myeloma and lymphoma and keeps them in remission longer."}}]}</script>
