<body data-page="service"><?php $nb_active = 'service'; $nb_current = '/bone-marrow-transplant'; include __DIR__ . '/includes/header.php'; ?>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="phero" aria-labelledby="b-title">
      <div class="wrap phero__in">
        <div>
          <ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/#services">Services</a></li><li>Bone Marrow Transplant</li></ol>
          <h1 id="b-title" data-fx="wipe">Bone Marrow Transplant in <span class="grad-text">Bangalore</span></h1>
          <p class="phero__sub" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.15s">A bone marrow transplant (BMT), also called a stem cell transplant, replaces diseased or damaged bone marrow with healthy blood-forming stem cells. It can cure conditions like thalassemia, aplastic anemia and some leukemias, and helps control others like myeloma and lymphoma.</p>
          <div class="hero__acts" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.25s">
            <a class="btn btn--grad" href="/contact?topic=bone-marrow-transplant">Book a consultation<svg class="ic ic--go"><use href="#i-go"/></svg></a>
            <a class="btn btn--line" href="tel:+917899756677"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-phone"/></svg>+91 78997 56677</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="phero__pic phero__pic--art" data-fx="iris" style="--d:.15s">
          <img src="/assets/images/illustrations/bmt-cells.svg" alt="Illustration of a stem cell inside bone marrow giving rise to new blood cells, representing a bone marrow transplant" width="400" height="400" fetchpriority="high">
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="bt-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="bt-what" data-fx="wipe">What is a <span class="grad-text">bone marrow transplant?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Bone marrow is the soft, spongy tissue inside your bones. It contains stem cells that make all your blood cells. When the bone marrow is damaged by disease, or when high-dose treatment is needed to destroy a cancer, healthy stem cells can be given to rebuild it.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">In a bone marrow transplant, healthy stem cells are given into a vein, much like a blood transfusion. <b>It is not surgery.</b> The stem cells travel to the bone marrow on their own and start making new red cells, white cells and platelets. This is called engraftment.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Dr. Neema Bhat, a US-trained hematologist and bone marrow transplant specialist with 10+ years of experience and 100+ transplants, offers bone marrow transplant in Bangalore for adults and children.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg>Where the stem cells come from</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Your own body</b> (autologous transplant)</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>A donor</b>, such as a brother, sister, parent or an unrelated volunteer (allogeneic transplant)</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Today, most stem cells are collected from the blood, not directly from the bone marrow. The term “bone marrow transplant” is still used for all types.</p><p class="box__sub">The need is large: India has about 1 to 1.5 lakh children with thalassemia major, and 10,000 to 15,000 more are born every year (Ministry of Health). For many of these children, a transplant is the only cure. Timely bone marrow transplant in Bangalore, done at the right stage of disease, gives the best chance of success.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="bt-who">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Who needs it</p><h2 class="title" id="bt-who" data-fx="wipe">Who needs a <span class="grad-text">bone marrow transplant?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">A bone marrow transplant may be advised for these conditions:</p>
        </div><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg>Blood cancers</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), <a href="/leukemia-treatment">leukemia</a></span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) not controlled by other treatment</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin <a href="/lymphoma-treatment">lymphoma</a> that has relapsed or is high-risk</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><a href="/multiple-myeloma-treatment">Multiple myeloma</a></span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) and myelofibrosis</span></li></ul></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg>Non-cancerous blood disorders</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><a href="/thalassemia-treatment">Thalassemia major</a></span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Sickle cell disease</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Severe aplastic anemia</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Other bone marrow failure syndromes, such as Fanconi anemia</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Immune and inherited disorders, mostly in children</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) and other primary immunodeficiencies</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD)</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>HLH (hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis)</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Some inherited metabolic disorders</span></li></ul></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Solid tumours (selected cases)</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Neuroblastoma in children</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Relapsed germ cell tumours</span></li></ul></div>
        </div><p class="box__sub">Not every patient with these conditions needs a transplant. The decision depends on the disease, its stage, how it responded to earlier treatment, your age and overall health, and whether a suitable donor is available. A transplant evaluation with a BMT specialist answers these questions.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="bt-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Types</p><h2 class="title" id="bt-types" data-fx="wipe">Types of bone marrow <span class="grad-text">transplant</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Where the stem cells come from</th><th>Mainly used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Autologous transplant</b></td><td data-label="Where the stem cells come from">Your own stem cells, collected and stored before high-dose treatment</td><td data-label="Mainly used for">Multiple myeloma, relapsed lymphoma, some solid tumours</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Matched sibling donor (allogeneic)</b></td><td data-label="Where the stem cells come from">A brother or sister with a full tissue (HLA) match</td><td data-label="Mainly used for">Thalassemia, aplastic anemia, leukemia, sickle cell disease</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Matched unrelated donor (allogeneic)</b></td><td data-label="Where the stem cells come from">An unrelated volunteer found through a stem cell donor registry</td><td data-label="Mainly used for">Leukemia, MDS, aplastic anemia, when no family match is available</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Haploidentical (half-matched)</b></td><td data-label="Where the stem cells come from">A parent, child, brother or sister who is a half match</td><td data-label="Mainly used for">Leukemia, aplastic anemia, thalassemia and others, when no full match is available</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Cord blood transplant</b></td><td data-label="Where the stem cells come from">Stem cells from a newborn’s umbilical cord, stored in a cord blood bank</td><td data-label="Mainly used for">Mainly children, in selected cases</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>Myeloablative conditioning</h3><p class="box__sub">Before a transplant, you receive conditioning treatment with chemotherapy, and sometimes radiation. Myeloablative (full-intensity) conditioning uses high doses to fully clear the bone marrow. It is mostly used for younger, fitter patients.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Reduced-intensity conditioning</h3><p class="box__sub">Uses lower doses and relies more on the donor’s immune cells to fight the disease. It allows older patients or those with other health problems to have a transplant.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="bt-donor">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="bt-donor"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Finding a donor: HLA matching</h3><p class="box__sub">For a donor transplant, the donor’s tissue type must match the patient’s. This is checked with a simple blood test or cheek swab called HLA typing (human leukocyte antigen typing).</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Brothers and sisters:</b> each full sibling has a 1 in 4 chance of being a full match.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Parents and children:</b> are usually a half match, so they can be donors for a haploidentical transplant.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Unrelated donors:</b> can be searched in Indian and international stem cell donor registries.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">A close match lowers the risk of rejection and graft-versus-host disease (GVHD). Thanks to newer methods, a half-matched family member is now a safe option for many patients who do not have a full match.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Is donating stem cells safe?</h3><p class="box__sub">Yes. Most donors give stem cells from the blood, through a process called apheresis. The donor first gets a few days of injections to move stem cells into the blood. Blood is then drawn from one arm, the stem cells are separated by a machine, and the rest of the blood is returned through the other arm.</p><p class="box__sub">It takes about 4 to 6 hours and needs no surgery. The donor’s body replaces the stem cells within a few weeks.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="bt-proc">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">The process</p><h2 class="title" id="bt-proc" data-fx="wipe">The transplant process, <span class="grad-text">step by step</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Transplant evaluation</b><p>Blood tests, heart, lung, kidney and liver tests, imaging, infection screening and a dental check-up to make sure you are fit for transplant.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Donor search and HLA typing</b><p>For donor transplants, family members are tested first, then registries if needed.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Central line placement</b><p>A thin tube (catheter) is placed in a large vein in the chest to give medicines, blood and stem cells, and to take blood samples.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Stem cell collection</b><p>Stem cells are collected from you (autologous) or from your donor, usually from the blood by apheresis.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Conditioning</b><p>About 5 to 10 days of chemotherapy, sometimes with radiation, to clear the diseased marrow and prepare the body.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Transplant day (Day 0)</b><p>The stem cells are given through the central line, like a blood transfusion. It usually takes less than an hour to a few hours.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Engraftment</b><p>New stem cells start making blood cells in about 10 to 14 days after an autologous transplant and 2 to 4 weeks after a donor transplant. You stay in a clean, HEPA-filtered BMT room.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>Discharge</b><p>Once blood counts recover and you can eat and drink well, usually about 2 to 3 weeks for an autologous and 4 to 6 weeks for a donor transplant.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Follow-up and recovery</b><p>Frequent visits in the first 100 days, then regular long-term check-ups.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="bt-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="bt-tx" data-fx="wipe">Bone marrow transplant <span class="grad-text">treatment options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Treatment is planned around your disease, its stage, your age, your fitness and the donor available. Options include:</p>
        </div><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Autologous stem cell transplant</h3><p>Your own stem cells are collected, stored and returned after high-dose chemotherapy.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Tandem (double) autologous transplant</h3><p>Two planned autologous transplants a few months apart, for selected myeloma patients.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Matched sibling donor transplant</h3><p>Stem cells from a fully matched brother or sister.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-link"/></svg><h3>Matched unrelated donor transplant</h3><p>Stem cells from a matched volunteer donor found through a registry.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Haploidentical (half-matched) transplant</h3><p>Stem cells from a half-matched parent, child, brother or sister.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg><h3>Cord blood transplant</h3><p>Stem cells from stored umbilical cord blood, in selected cases.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><h3>Myeloablative conditioning</h3><p>Full-intensity chemotherapy, with or without radiation, before transplant.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><h3>Reduced-intensity conditioning</h3><p>Lower-dose conditioning for older patients or those with other health problems.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Total body irradiation (TBI)</h3><p>Radiation to the whole body as part of conditioning, for selected conditions.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>GVHD prevention and treatment</h3><p>Immune-suppressing therapy to stop donor cells from attacking the patient’s body.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Donor lymphocyte infusion (DLI)</h3><p>Extra immune cells from the donor to treat relapse or boost the donor’s cells.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Infection prevention</h3><p>HEPA-filtered rooms, preventive antibiotics, antivirals and antifungals, and regular monitoring.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Blood and platelet transfusions</h3><p>To support you until the new marrow starts working.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-apple"/></svg><h3>Nutrition support</h3><p>A dietitian-planned diet, and feeding support if eating is difficult.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Vaccination after transplant</h3><p>Childhood and adult vaccines are repeated, as the transplant resets the immune system.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-adult"/></svg><h3>Physiotherapy and rehabilitation</h3><p>To rebuild strength and stamina.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Counselling and support</h3><p>Psychological support for patients, donors and families through the transplant journey.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Long-term follow-up care</h3><p>Checks for late effects on the heart, lungs, hormones, bones, eyes and fertility.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="bt-risk">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Risks</p><h2 class="title" id="bt-risk" data-fx="wipe">Risks and side effects of <span class="grad-text">bone marrow transplant</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">A bone marrow transplant is a major treatment. Your transplant team watches closely for these risks and treats them early:</p>
        </div><ul class="conds"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg>Infections</b><p>The most common risk while blood counts are low, and for months after a donor transplant.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Mouth sores, nausea, diarrhoea</b><p>From conditioning chemotherapy. These usually settle as counts recover.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg>Low blood counts</b><p>You may need red cell and platelet transfusions until the new marrow works.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD)</b><p>After a donor transplant, the donor’s immune cells may attack the skin, gut or liver. It is treated with immune-suppressing medicines.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg>Graft failure</b><p>Rarely, the new stem cells do not engraft and further treatment is needed.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>Organ problems</b><p>Such as liver (veno-occlusive disease), kidney, lung or heart problems.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Infertility</b><p>Conditioning can affect fertility. Ask about sperm, egg or embryo freezing before treatment.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg>Relapse</b><p>The original disease may come back in some patients.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg>Late effects</b><p>Cataracts, thyroid and hormone changes, bone thinning, and a small risk of second cancers years later.</p></li></ul><p class="alert" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><span><b>Call your transplant team urgently</b> if you have a fever of 100.4°F (38°C) or more, chills, breathlessness, bleeding, severe diarrhoea, a new rash or yellow eyes after a transplant.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="bt-life">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="bt-life"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Recovery and life after a bone marrow transplant</h3><p class="box__sub">Recovery takes time. Blood counts usually recover in weeks, but the immune system can take 6 months to 1 year or more to rebuild, especially after a donor transplant. These steps help:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Stay close to the hospital for the first 100 days if you live far away, so that check-ups are easy.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent infections:</b> wash hands often, wear a mask in crowded places, and avoid people with colds, fever or chickenpox.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Eat safe food:</b> freshly cooked, hot food and boiled or filtered water. Avoid raw salads, street food, cut fruit kept outside and unpasteurised milk until your doctor allows.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Take every medicine on time, especially immune-suppressing and anti-infection medicines. Never stop them on your own.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Protect your skin from the sun, as GVHD and some medicines make the skin sensitive.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Stay gently active: short walks help rebuild strength.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Get your vaccines again on the schedule your transplant team gives you.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Return to work or school gradually, usually within a few months, as advised.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Most transplant survivors return to normal daily life. Children go back to school, and adults return to work and family life.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

